9918               
9919               /IN PRINTING TO DRAFT PRINTERS, VERY LITTLE IS PRESUMED ABOUT THE
9920               /ATTACHED PRINTER.  WE ASSUME THAT CARRIAGE RETURN RETURNS THE
9921               /CARRIAGE BUT DOES NOT IMPLY A LINE FEED.  BACKSPACE IS USED ONLY
9922               /TO IMPLEMENT DEAD KEY SEQUENCES.  THUS, IN GENERAL, THIS ROUTINE
9923               /USES A MULTIPLE PASS STRATEGY TO PRINT THE LINE.  COMMENTS AT
9924               /DPLIN3 FURTHER EXPLAIN THE MULTIPLE PASS STRATEGY
9925               
9926               
9927               /RESET ALL POINTERS. THE UPPER FIVE (5) BITS OF EACH WORD
9928               /IN THE LINE HAVE BEEN PROCESSED.
9929               /THIS IS THE START OF THE LINE.
9930               
9931 031201* 5777  DPLIN2, JMP     LPRSTP  /RESET THE POINTERS, RETURN TO DPLIN3
9932               
9933               /THIS INNER LOOP PROCESSES EACH CHARACTER IN THE LINE, AND ACTS
9934               /ACCORDING TO EACH CHARACTER'S MODE BITS.
9935               
9936               /A) IF NONE OF A CHARACTER'S MODE BITS ARE SET, THIS IS THE LAST PASS DURING
9937               /WHICH IT WILL PRINT.  IT IS REPLACED IN THE BUFFER BY A SPACE.
9938               
9939               
9940               /B) IF A CHARACTER IS UNDERLINED, AN UNDERSCORE IS PRINTED INSTEAD
9941               /OF THE CHARACTER UNTIL THE PROPER UNDERLINE DARKNESS IS ACHIEVED.
9942               /THEN THE UNDERLINE ATTRIBUTE IS DELETED AND THE CHARACTER ITSELF
9943               /PRINTS NORMALLY ON SUBSEQUENT PASSES.
9944               
9945               /C) THE DARKNESS REQUIRED WILL DETERMINE HOW MANY PASSES THROUGH THE LINE
9946               /WILL BE DONE. THE HIT BITS (BITS 1&2 OF THE WORD) DETERMINE THE PASSES.
9947               /00= ONE PASS, 01= TWO PASSES, 10= THREE PASSES, 11= FOUR PASSES.
9948               
9949               /D) IF A CHARACTER IS PART OF A DEAD KEY SEQUENCE, EACH CHARACTER
9950               /IN THE SEQUENCE IS TREATED SEPERATELY.  IF A CHARACTER IN A DEAD KEY
9951               /SEQUENCE IS REPLACED WITH A SPACE AND THAT IS NOT THE LAST PASS,
9952               /WE PAY A SMALL SPEED PENALTY.
9953               
9954               /IF A CHARACTER FALLS INTO ANY CATEGORY OTHER THAN A), ANOTHER PASS IS
9955               /NECESSARY.  EVENTUALLY, ALL CHARACTERS WILL FALL INTO CATEGORY A) AND
9956               /THE LINE WILL BE COMPLETED.
9957               
9958               
9959 031202* 1436  DPLIN3, TAD I   PLPTR   /GET THE FIRST CHARACTER IN ITS POSITION
9960 031203* 7450          SNA             /FLUSH NULL CHARACTERS. THEY CAN'T BE PROCESSED.
9961 031204* 5776          JMP     DPLIN9  /GET NEXT CHARACTER
9962               
9963               /WE SAVE THE CHARACTER WITHOUT MODE BITS IN THE AC
9964               
9965 031205* 0375          AND     (4177)  /MASK OUT THE MODE BITS
9966 031206* 7421          MQL             /SAVE THE MODE-BIT-LESS CHARACTER IN THE MQ
9967               
9968               /WE NOW MUST STRIP ALL THE ATTRIBUTES (AFTER ALL THIS WORK!)            /A214
9969               /IF WE ARE PRINTING TO A REAL HOST. (AS OPPOSED TO A PRINTER            /A214
9970               /CONNECTED TO THE HOST PORT).                                           /A214
9971               
9972 031207* 1774          TAD     PRQPNT          /GET DOCUMENT DESTINATION               /A214
9973                       TAD     (-DHOST2)       /                                       /A214
undefined       	   	       ^
undefined       	   	       ^
     031210* 1373  
9974 031211* 7640          SZA CLA                 /SKIP IF A REAL HOST                    /A214
9975 031212* 5215          JMP     DPLIN4          /REAL PRINTER                           /A214
9976 031213* 7501          MQA                     /GET MODELESS CHR                       /A214
9977 031214* 3436          DCA I   PLPTR           /SAVE BACK IN THE BUFFER                /A214
9978               
9979               /NOW CHECK FOR THE CODE 21, 22, OR 23. THIS IS THE SEQUENCE FOR
9980               /PRINTING A TECHNICAL CHARACTER IN ANOTHER CHARACTER SET. IT ALSO CHANGES TO
9981               /G2 OR G3. THE CHARACTER STRING WILL BE TWO CHARACTERS. THE FIRST WILL BE THE
9982               /CONTROL CHARACTER (21,22,OR 23), THE NEXT WILL BE THE CHARACTER OR THE "G"
9983               /SET DESIRED. NOTE THAT THIS TWO CHARACTER STRING IS NOT CALLED A "G" STRING
9984               /BUT IT COULD BE IF YOU SO DESIRE. THE CHARACTER ATTRIBUTES WILL ALSO BE
9985               /ON THE CONTROL CHARACTER.
9986               
9987 031215* 1436  DPLIN4, TAD I   PLPTR   /NOW CHECK TO SEE IF THE CHARACTER IS A CONTROL
9988                       AND     P177    /CHARACTER THAT PRECEDES THE TECHNICAL CHARACTER.
