10898               
10899               /THE SAVE AND RESTORE ROUTINES ARE USED TO HANDLE HEADERS AND FOOTERS.  THE
10900               /ESSENTIAL IDEA IS THAT WHEN A HEADER OR FOOTER PRINT CONTROL BLOCK IS
10901               /ENCOUNTERED, THE STATE OF THE PRINTER IS RECORDED.  IT IS RECORDED IN
10902               /SUCH A WAY THAT THAT STATE CAN BE RESTORED LATER AT ANY TIME.
10903               
10904               /THEN, WHEN IT COMES TIME TO PRINT A HEADER/FOOTER, THE CURRENT STATE OF
10905               /THE PRINTER IS SAVED, AND THE APPROPRIATE SAVED STATE IS RESTORED.  IN THIS
10906               /STATE, THE PRINTER CAN PRINT THE HEADER/FOOTER.  WHEN THE HEADER/FOOTER
10907               /COMPLETES, THE OLD CURRENT STATE IS RESTORED, AND PRINTING RESUMES WHERE
10908               /IT LEFT OFF.
10909               
10910               /THE STATE OF THE PRINTER CAN BE DIVIDED INTO TWO PARTS:
10911               
10912               /1.  PRINTER VARIABLES
10913               /2.  POSITION IN THE FILE
10914               
10915               /THE PRINTER VARIABLES WHICH MUST BE SAVED DURING A STATE SAVE ARE:
10916               
10917               /1.  PRRLSZ - THE LINE SIZE SETTING MANDATED BY THE RULER.  THIS IS SAVED
10918               /             SO THAT THE RULER IN EFFECT AT THE TIME OF THE SAVE CAN TAKE
10919               /             EFFECT AGAIN.
10920               
10921               /2.  PRRM - THE JUSTIFICATION SETTING MANDATED BY THE RULER.  AGAIN, THIS
10922               /           IS SAVED SO THAT THE RULER IN EFFECT AT THE TIME OF THE SAVE CAN
10923               /           TAKE EFFECT AGAIN.
10924               
10925               /3.  PRGCBK - THE BACKUP LOCATION FOR THE READ ROUTINE, PRGCHR.  THIS IS
10926               /             SAVED SO THAT ANY BACKED UP READ IS STILL BACKED UP WHEN
10927               /             THE STATE IS RESTORED.
10928               
10929               
10930               /THE POSITION IN THE FILE MUST ALSO BE SAVED.  THE IDEA HERE IS THAT THE
10931               /STATE OF THE FILE SYSTEM IS SAVED, SO THAT WE CAN LATER MOVE TO THE
10932               /APPROPRIATE PART OF THE FILE WHEN IT IS TIME TO RESTORE.  THIS WORKS
10933               /QUITE NICELY WHEN READING FROM A FILE, SINCE IT IS POSSIBLE TO BACK UP
10934               /IN A FILE.  HOWEVER, WHEN READING CHARACTERS FROM THE EDITOR UNDER LIST
10935               /PROCESSING, IT IS NOT POSSIBLE TO BACK UP.  THEREFORE, TWO STRATEGIES ARE
10936               /EMPLOYED; ONE FOR FILE READING, ONE FOR LIST PROCESSING.
10937               
10938               /1.  FILE SYSTEM SAVE/RESTORE IS HANDLED JOINTLY BY THE PRINTER AND THE FILE
10939               /    SYSTEM.  FOUR VARIABLES WITHIN THE FILE SYSTEM DEFINE THE STATE OF THE
10940               /    FILE READ; THESE VARIABLES ARE REMOTELY SAVED AND RESTORED BY THE
10941               /    PRINTER.  WHEN RESTORING, TWO ROUTINES MUST BE INVOKED FOLLOWING THE
10942               /    VARIABLE RESTORATION TO FILL THE BUFFERS BACK UP.  THE ADVANTAGE OF THIS
10943               /    SCHEME IS THAT THERE IS NO RESTRICTION OF HEADER/FOOTER LENGTH.
10944               
10945               /2.  LIST PROCESSING SAVE/RESTORE IS HANDLED SOLELY BY THE PRINTER.  WHEN
10946               /    THE HEADER/FOOTER BLOCK IS ENCOUNTERED, IT IS READ CHARACTER BY
10947               /    CHARACTER AND WRITTEN OUT TO A SPECIAL DISK BUFFER.  WHEN THE HEADER/
10948               /    FOOTER IS RESTORED, THE CHARACTERS ARE READ BACK FROM THE DISK.  HENCE,
10949               /    DURING LIST PROCESSING THE PRINTER MUST READ FROM THE EDITOR WHEN
10950               /    PRINTING NORMAL TEXT, AND FROM THE DISK BUFFER WHEN PROCESSING A
10951               /    HEADER/FOOTER.  UNDER THIS SCHEME THE LENGTH OF A HEADER OR FOOTER,
10952               /    INCLUDING RULERS, IS LIMITED TO FIVE BLOCKS.
10953               
10954               
10955               /THE ALERT READER MAY WONDER WHY THE TWO CASES ARE TREATED SEPARATELY.  A
10956               /FIVE BLOCK RESTRICTION IS NOT SEVERE, SO WHY NOT BUFFER ALL HEADER/FOOTERS
10957               /ON DISK?  WHEN DOING LIST PROCESSING, THE SCROLL BUFFERS ARE NOT IN USE,
10958               /AND THESE ARE USED TO BUFFER HEADER/FOOTERS BEFORE WRITING TO DISK.  THERE
10959               /IS NO SUITIABLE BUFFER TO USE IN THE GENERAL CASE.
10960               
10961               
10962               /THIS ROUTINE SAVES THE STATE OF THE PRINTER AND FILE SYSTEM.  IT IS USED
10963               /TO "REMEMBER" HEADERS AND FOOTERS SO THAT THEY CAN BE PRINTED LATER AND
10964               /TO "REMEMBER" OUR PLACE WHEN WE PRINT HEADERS/FOOTERS SO THAT WE CAN
10965               /PICK UP WHERE WE LEFT OFF LATER.
10966               
10967               /THE ARGUMENT TO THIS ROUTINE IS ZERO IF THE STATE IS TO BE SAVED FOR
10968               /RESTORATION AS THE CURRENT LOCATION; MINUS ONE IF THE STATE IS TO BE
10969               /SAVED FOR RESTORATION AS A FOOTER; AND ONE IF THE STATE IS TO BE SAVED
10970               /FOR RESTORATION AS A HEADER.
10971               
10972               /AC=TYPE OF SAVE
10973               /JMS    PRSAVE
10974               /AC=0
10975               
10976               
